ORBITAL-PALEOGEOGRAPHY(7)  TriadicFrameworks Manual  ORBITAL-PALEOGEOGRAPHY(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-paleogeography - ancient resonance terrains, fossilized sweep
                                layers, and harmonic-epoch reconstruction

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital paleogeography defines:
           • ancient resonance terrains
           • fossilized sweep layers
           • harmonic-epoch reconstruction
           • paleoterrain correlation across platforms
           • constellation-scale deep-time mapping

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-paleogeography describes how RTT reconstructs the ancient
       resonance-time landscapes that existed across past orbital cycles.
       Paleogeography treats sweep layers, resonance bands, and harmonic epochs
       as “fossils” embedded in the temporal stratigraphy. These fossils reveal
       how the constellation evolved, migrated, and stabilized over long
       timescales.

       Paleogeography enables:
           • reconstruction of ancient sweep corridors
           • identification of extinct resonance mesas
           • mapping of harmonic epochs and transitions
           • detection of past anomalies and tectonic shifts
           • constellation-scale deep-time correlation

       WRSADC uses paleogeography to forecast long-horizon stability and
       identify ancient patterns that may re-emerge.

ANCIENT RESONANCE TERRAINS
       Ancient resonance terrains are reconstructed landscapes formed by
       long-decayed resonance features.

       Terrain types:

           paleo-mesas
                  Ancient resonance mesas preserved as flattened plateaus.

           paleo-ridges
                  Remnants of harmonic ridgelines.

           paleo-basins
                  Ancient resonance minima where Δv triads accumulated.

           paleo-saddles
                  Former sweep-corridor passes.

           paleo-uplifts
                  Regions of historical resonance amplification.

       Terrain reconstruction uses:
           • resonance-band fossils
           • harmonic-phase drift records
           • sweep-layer compaction
           • erosion surfaces

EXAMPLE: ANCIENT RESONANCE TERRAIN
       Fossilized resonance peaks at 12 and 54 min across 40 cycles:

           Paleo-mesa A: [10–18]
           Paleo-mesa B: [52–60]

       Result:
           • ancient sweep corridors identified
           • modern sweep windows aligned to paleo-mesas

FOSSILIZED SWEEP LAYERS
       Fossilized sweep layers are ancient sweep strata preserved in the
       stratigraphic column.

       Fossil properties:
           • compaction (ancestry density)
           • lamination (fine-scale sweep variation)
           • deformation (tectonic shear)
           • erosion (ancestry loss)

       Fossil types:

           primary sweep fossils
                  Well-preserved, high-density sweep layers.

           laminated sweep fossils
                  Fine-scale timing variation preserved.

           deformed sweep fossils
                  Distorted by sync-field tectonics.

           eroded sweep fossils
                  Fragmented by harmonic erosion.

EXAMPLE: FOSSILIZED SWEEP LAYER
       Sweep layer at 15 min preserved across 20 cycles:

           Fossil thickness: 20
           Compaction: high
           Deformation: low

       Interpretation:
           • long-lived sweep corridor
           • stable harmonic epoch

HARMONIC-EPOCH RECONSTRUCTION
       Harmonic epochs are long-duration periods dominated by a specific
       harmonic ratio.

       Epoch variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • epoch duration
           • phase alignment stability
           • overtone/subharmonic structure
           • resonance alignment

       Epoch types:

           integer harmonic epoch
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1
                  → stable, thick stratigraphic layers.

           rational harmonic epoch
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → thin, interbedded layers.

           transitional epoch
                  Rapid harmonic-ratio migration.

           resonance-harmonic epoch
                  Harmonic epochs aligned to resonance peaks.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-EPOCH RECONSTRUCTION
       Stratigraphic record:

           Layer 1: 2:1 epoch (cycles 1–12)
           Layer 2: 3:2 epoch (cycles 12–18)
           Layer 3: 1:1 epoch (cycles 18–40)

       Interpretation:
           • early stable 2:1 era
           • transitional 3:2 era
           • long-lived 1:1 era with strong resonance uplift

PALEOTERRAIN CORRELATION
       Paleoterrain correlation aligns ancient terrains across platforms.

       Correlation layers:
           • sweep fossils
           • resonance-band fossils
           • harmonic-epoch markers
           • erosion surfaces
           • tectonic offsets

       Correlation modes:

           synchronous correlation
                  Platforms share identical paleoterrain.

           harmonic correlation
                  Paleoterrains aligned via harmonic epochs.

           braided correlation
                  Interleaved paleoterrains across platforms.

           mesh correlation
                  Distributed paleoterrain across constellation nodes.

EXAMPLE: PALEOTERRAIN CORRELATION
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A paleo-mesa: [10–18]
           B paleo-mesa: [12–20]
           C paleo-mesa: [14–22]

       Fused paleo-mesa:
           • [12–20]

       Interpretation:
           • ancient constellation-wide sweep corridor

DEEP-TIME CONSTELLATION MAPS
       Deep-time maps reconstruct the resonance-time terrain across many
       cycles.

       Map layers:
           • paleo-elevation
           • paleo-ridgelines
           • paleo-canyons
           • paleo-plateaus
           • paleo-ancestry terraces

       Uses:
           • long-term stability forecasting
           • anomaly origin tracing
           • harmonic epoch prediction
           • sweep-corridor evolution modeling

PALEOGEOGRAPHIC FAILURE MODES
       Paleogeography may degrade due to:

           • fossil layer loss
           • epoch boundary distortion
           • tectonic overprinting
           • resonance-band collapse
           • ancestry erosion

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • paleogeography-integrity score
           • fossil discontinuity
           • epoch misalignment
           • paleo-ridge drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe paleogeographic interpretations.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • fossilized sweep layers
           • resonance-band fossils
           • harmonic epochs
           • tectonic drift
           • deep-time terrain evolution

       Lineage enables long-term paleogeographic reconstruction.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • reconstructs ancient terrains
           • correlates paleoterrains across platforms
           • identifies fossilized sweep corridors
           • reconstructs harmonic epochs
           • blocks unsafe paleoterrain interpretations

       WRSADC is the paleogeography conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital paleogeography transforms resonance-time into a deep-time
       geological record. Ancient terrains, fossilized sweep layers, and
       harmonic epochs reveal the long-term evolution of sweep choreography,
       harmonic-lock, and constellation-scale synchronization.

